1
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:17,370
PHILIP GREENSPUN: There
is no pizza for me

2
00:00:17,370 --> 00:00:19,037
because I'm going to
talk about Oshkosh.

3
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:25,800
So what is it?

4
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,430
It's end of July every year
at a big, a physically big,

5
00:00:29,430 --> 00:00:33,030
but otherwise not very
busy airport in Wisconsin.

6
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:36,900
And about 10,000
airplanes come in.

7
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:37,980
People camp out.

8
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:42,450
People hang out, usually at
least 100,000 people a day.

9
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:43,710
There's a Boeing.

10
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:46,500
There's a C-17 cargo plane.

11
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,960
A lot of experimental-- it's
founded by the Experimental

12
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,123
Aircraft Association.

13
00:00:50,123 --> 00:00:51,540
So there's a lot
of people who are

14
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:53,082
passionate about
home-building there.

15
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:57,810
You can see all the
airplanes parked.

16
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:06,970
There's 12,000 camping
sites, 40,000 campers.

17
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:09,490
There's 1,500
workshops and seminars

18
00:01:09,490 --> 00:01:10,720
that you can take to learn.

19
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:13,450
Or you can just walk around and
talk to people, and hang out.

20
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:16,420
So I planned this trip
for me and my friend Ray.

21
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:20,560
He's a helicopter
pilot from Ireland.

22
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:23,530
And the idea was to
fly to Niagara Falls

23
00:01:23,530 --> 00:01:25,540
and show him Niagara
Falls, and on the way

24
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:27,430
back go up to Mackinac Island.

25
00:01:27,430 --> 00:01:30,710
They saw automobiles about
120 years ago and they said,

26
00:01:30,710 --> 00:01:31,970
this is a terrible technology.

27
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:33,370
It'll ruin everything.

28
00:01:33,370 --> 00:01:34,240
They banned cars.

29
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:35,440
The only powered air--

30
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,670
the only powered vehicle you can
legally have on Mackinac Island

31
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:41,530
is an aircraft.

32
00:01:41,530 --> 00:01:42,910
They do have an airport.

33
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:47,080
Anyway, on the back, we were
going to go to Toronto as well.

34
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:50,620
So we had to get all this stuff
ready for international flying.

35
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:53,620
I had it all
carefully planned out

36
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:56,710
where we were going to
have 540 pounds of stuff,

37
00:01:56,710 --> 00:02:00,280
which is right at the payload
of the airplane with full fuel.

38
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:04,490
But then Oxana,
who was just here--

39
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:07,790
she's an aerobatic pilot at
East Coast Aero Club-- she

40
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:09,590
said she wanted to come too.

41
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:12,620
So then we had to
add a fuel stop.

42
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:15,438
There's our merry
band of three people.

43
00:02:15,438 --> 00:02:17,480
There's a whole bunch of
camping gear in the back

44
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:19,490
that you can't quite see.

45
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:23,960
Oshkosh becomes temporarily the
busiest airport in the world.

46
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,940
And you might say,
well, how can that be?

47
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:27,110
O'Hare has more flights.

48
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:28,250
But they are only--

49
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,530
Oshkosh is only open
about 10 hours a day

50
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:33,470
because they close
the airport at night.

51
00:02:33,470 --> 00:02:37,040
And they close it also for
a few hours every afternoon

52
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:39,620
for an airshow.

53
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:46,920
This is somebody's iPad,
I think, a screen capture

54
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:49,680
of airplanes flying
in and around Oshkosh.

55
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:51,810
And these are only the
ones with the newest

56
00:02:51,810 --> 00:02:54,460
ADS-B out transponders.

57
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:56,940
So it doesn't show, probably--

58
00:02:56,940 --> 00:03:00,682
this is probably only 20%
of the total airplanes.

59
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:06,030
You have special arrival rules.

60
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:10,170
If you're not a
sissy, then you have

61
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:13,405
to fly in visually, circle
around until it's your turn.

62
00:03:13,405 --> 00:03:15,780
And then they'll tell you you
can't use the whole runway.

63
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:17,363
You have to land on
one of these dots.

64
00:03:17,363 --> 00:03:20,540
They'll say, land
on the green dot.

65
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:24,500
If you are a sissy, you file
IFR, get your IFR reservation

66
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:26,600
with this DOS grade
computer system,

67
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,110
confirm it with this DOS
grade computer system

68
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:31,070
that nobody else
wants to deal with,

69
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:33,590
and then you get the whole
runway, and you just land.

70
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:34,090
All right.

71
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:37,630
Here's a picture of the
multifunction display.

72
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:40,040
No, actually, I guess this
was in FlightAware later.

73
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,380
This shows that
we diverted a bit.

74
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:45,170
From Bedford, we ended up going
up to Watertown, New York,

75
00:03:45,170 --> 00:03:45,920
so we didn't--

76
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:47,965
we could have-- legally,
we're instrument rated

77
00:03:47,965 --> 00:03:48,590
and everything.

78
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:52,550
We could have flown
through these rain showers.

79
00:03:52,550 --> 00:03:56,160
But it's always smarter just
to go around if you can.

80
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:56,750
OK.

81
00:03:56,750 --> 00:03:58,340
So we flew over Niagara Falls.

82
00:03:58,340 --> 00:03:59,630
We landed.

83
00:03:59,630 --> 00:04:01,430
We had that whole
runway to ourself,

84
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:03,920
about 10,000-foot runway.

85
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:06,580
And it's actually
oriented east-west.

86
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:07,730
So we actually just--

87
00:04:07,730 --> 00:04:10,280
essentially, we could have
done the same heading basically

88
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:12,860
from Hanscom Field all the
way to the end of the runway

89
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:14,630
and where we turned off.

90
00:04:14,630 --> 00:04:15,230
You get there.

91
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:16,215
And there's just--

92
00:04:16,215 --> 00:04:20,180
I don't know-- 30 P-51 Mustangs
right next to your tent.

93
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:24,830
There are people building this
One Week Wonder, an RV kit,

94
00:04:24,830 --> 00:04:26,060
where you get to pull rivets.

95
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:27,230
Oxana is actually here.

96
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:30,890
And she can tell you
about her experience

97
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:32,380
helping to build that airplane.

98
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:35,830
There you see your B1 bomber.

99
00:04:35,830 --> 00:04:37,990
I love the antiques.

100
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:41,320
Here's one from the late '20s,
a Lincoln-Page airplane that

101
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:43,600
has been recently
restored and flying.

102
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:48,310
There's that replica autogyro,
a beautiful Cessna 170.

103
00:04:48,310 --> 00:04:52,065
That's an old Piper
Comanche there on the right.

104
00:04:52,065 --> 00:04:53,940
There's not a whole lot
of helicopter action.

105
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:54,880
There's a little bit.

106
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,905
This is what's called a
Safari Helicopter Kit.

107
00:04:57,905 --> 00:04:59,280
That's how you
know that you have

108
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:01,870
real confidence in your
mechanical abilities

109
00:05:01,870 --> 00:05:05,160
if you build and then
fly your own helicopter.

110
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:06,180
Piper Cub.

111
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:09,390
This is a guy who stuck
a Pratt and Whitney--

112
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:10,560
I think it's a Pratt--

113
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:16,230
PT6 engine into a Polish Wilga,
a short field landing airplane.

114
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:17,360
He added fuel tanks.

115
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,410
It's such a fuel
hog now that he had

116
00:05:19,410 --> 00:05:22,770
to add extra fuel
capacity in the struts

117
00:05:22,770 --> 00:05:27,730
and flies all around the west,
going camping with his family.

118
00:05:27,730 --> 00:05:28,230
All right.

119
00:05:28,230 --> 00:05:29,725
And that's called a Travel Air.

120
00:05:29,725 --> 00:05:30,850
There are a bunch of these.

121
00:05:30,850 --> 00:05:32,730
So these are airplanes
from the '20s.

122
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:35,310
People were just using
them for transportation,

123
00:05:35,310 --> 00:05:38,520
throwing a friend in the
back maybe and a tent

124
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:40,530
and going and camping
out at Oshkosh.

125
00:05:40,530 --> 00:05:43,470
So I think that's pretty cool.

126
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:49,140
There are only five of these
Piaggio seaplanes, P136s,

127
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:55,530
that still exist out of 63 that
were built from 1948 to 1961.

128
00:05:55,530 --> 00:05:58,050
I saw two of them parked next
to each other at Oshkosh.

129
00:05:58,050 --> 00:06:02,550
That's what makes it
a unique experience.

130
00:06:02,550 --> 00:06:06,270
Here's some kind of airplane
doing a simulated bombing run.

131
00:06:06,270 --> 00:06:09,390
Meanwhile, a family is
there in their personal DC-3

132
00:06:09,390 --> 00:06:12,110
camping out.

133
00:06:12,110 --> 00:06:16,260
Here's our little plane and
the $99 tent from Costco.

134
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:20,960
It seemed like a great
idea at the time.

135
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,530
I think we have a slide on that.

136
00:06:22,530 --> 00:06:23,030
OK.

137
00:06:23,030 --> 00:06:24,440
This kind of summarizes it.

138
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:27,470
This is the unfortunate state
of general aviation now.

139
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:29,690
So we have the
innovation showcase.

140
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:32,480
And right in front of it, a
Piper Seminole, which I think

141
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:37,640
was certified in the 1970s
and certainly a derivative

142
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:40,370
of things going back a
lot farther than that.

143
00:06:40,370 --> 00:06:42,980
There were some
interesting talks there,

144
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:45,050
some heavy hitters.

145
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:48,110
This guy Pat Anderson is a
professor at Embry-Riddle.

146
00:06:48,110 --> 00:06:50,360
And he said actually
that we haven't really

147
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:52,340
seen anything in the
electric aircraft world

148
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:54,440
because in the
early days of jets,

149
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:57,205
they just took these piston
airframes and stuck a jet

150
00:06:57,205 --> 00:06:57,830
engine in them.

151
00:06:57,830 --> 00:06:59,600
And they didn't perform
that differently

152
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,130
from the piston fighters.

153
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:03,680
But then five years later,
they had the swept wings

154
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:06,710
and what we would recognize
as the modern fighter jet.

155
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:08,630
So it completely--
it was the jet

156
00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:11,120
engine did completely
transform the aircraft,

157
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:12,380
but not immediately.

158
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:14,200
So he said, you're
seeing some of this now,

159
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:16,430
where people take a
little two-seat trainer

160
00:07:16,430 --> 00:07:18,740
and they remove
the piston engine

161
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:20,780
and put an electric
motor with the propeller.

162
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:22,792
But that's not really--

163
00:07:22,792 --> 00:07:24,500
what we're really
going to see are things

164
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:25,792
that are much more like drones.

165
00:07:28,010 --> 00:07:29,293
There is an Uber.

166
00:07:29,293 --> 00:07:31,460
There was a guy from Uber
that was very interesting.

167
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:35,810
Uber has aeronautical
engineers working for them,

168
00:07:35,810 --> 00:07:38,540
designing aircraft, and then
releasing the designs for free,

169
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:40,550
hoping that people
will make them.

170
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:42,240
They're claiming that
they're going to--

171
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:43,250
I don't know.

172
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:45,380
They've got to pay somebody
off in the government

173
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:46,310
to make this happen.

174
00:07:46,310 --> 00:07:49,680
2023 they say they're going to
be operating in Los Angeles.

175
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:51,630
Their idea is that
the world is going

176
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:55,190
to be so congested that
people will pay up happily

177
00:07:55,190 --> 00:07:59,310
to hop over the congestion
in relatively short hops.

178
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:01,730
So Uber is putting a
lot of money into this.

179
00:08:01,730 --> 00:08:04,220
They have some partners
like Airbus, Pipistrel,

180
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:06,140
the Slovenian company.

181
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:08,100
We'll see what happens.

182
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:10,360
So some round-the-world
pilots were there.

183
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:12,860
Actually, I don't know how many
you guys know this, but Matt

184
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:14,690
Guthmiller, who was an MIT--

185
00:08:14,690 --> 00:08:16,250
between freshman
and sophomore years

186
00:08:16,250 --> 00:08:18,350
became the youngest
person ever to fly around

187
00:08:18,350 --> 00:08:21,560
the world in a
Beechcraft Bonanza.

188
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,000
I mean, he was in a
Beechcraft Bonanza

189
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,800
and he was the youngest person
to fly around the world.

190
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:32,260
But there's also-- at
Oshkosh, we met this guy

191
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:39,470
Dick Rutan who flew around
the world non-stop unrefueled

192
00:08:39,470 --> 00:08:44,400
in a plane designed by
his brother Burt Rutan.

193
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:48,360
What else is Burt Rutan famous
for besides designing aircraft?

194
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:49,650
Does anybody know?

195
00:08:49,650 --> 00:08:50,870
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

196
00:08:50,870 --> 00:08:52,650
PHILIP GREENSPUN: What?

197
00:08:52,650 --> 00:08:54,740
AUDIENCE: Wasn't he
on like a spacecraft?

198
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:56,490
PHILIP GREENSPUN:
Spacecraft, that's true.

199
00:08:56,490 --> 00:08:58,702
But yeah, spacecraft and
aircraft, but what else?

200
00:08:58,702 --> 00:09:00,670
AUDIENCE: Canard planes.

201
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:03,370
PHILIP GREENSPUN: Canard
planes, those are airplanes.

202
00:09:03,370 --> 00:09:05,320
Climate change denial.

203
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:06,430
Look it up.

204
00:09:06,430 --> 00:09:10,600
He's got like a 100-page book
about the scientific fraud

205
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:11,550
of climate change.

206
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:12,508
But anyway, Dick Rutan.

207
00:09:12,508 --> 00:09:16,150
And the other interesting thing
is before this nine-day flight,

208
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:20,020
he was engaged to be
married to Jeana Yeager

209
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:25,120
and supposedly what
I heard at Oshkosh

210
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:29,650
was that they have not spoken
since they landed in 1986.

211
00:09:29,650 --> 00:09:31,570
So in nine days, there
can be quite a change

212
00:09:31,570 --> 00:09:33,940
in a relationship apparently.

213
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:37,178
We met this pilot Shaesta Waiz.

214
00:09:37,178 --> 00:09:39,220
I don't know if that's
the correct pronunciation.

215
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:41,410
She grew up in
California and learned

216
00:09:41,410 --> 00:09:42,950
to fly relatively recently.

217
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:45,310
And she also flew a
Bonanza all the way

218
00:09:45,310 --> 00:09:48,390
around the world over 145 days.

219
00:09:48,390 --> 00:09:49,390
So that was interesting.

220
00:09:49,390 --> 00:09:52,010
She actually said it gets a
little bit easier once you're

221
00:09:52,010 --> 00:09:54,010
out over the ocean from
a weather point of view.

222
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:56,980
She said except for the
Intertropical Convergence

223
00:09:56,980 --> 00:09:58,990
of the Equator,
most weather is kind

224
00:09:58,990 --> 00:10:00,970
of a phenomenon of
being over land.

225
00:10:00,970 --> 00:10:03,370
To me, the most impressive
round the world pilot,

226
00:10:03,370 --> 00:10:06,550
though, was actually an
Italian guy that I met.

227
00:10:06,550 --> 00:10:08,620
He was in a Cirrus SR22.

228
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:11,700
And he just flies to
Oshkosh every year.

229
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:15,370
He just loads up in Italy,
turns the key, and goes.

230
00:10:15,370 --> 00:10:18,040
Every year, flies over
the Atlantic, and then

231
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:20,140
flies back to Italy
when it's over.

232
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:23,260
So I thought that is actually
the coolest thing, when

233
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:24,862
it's not even an adventure.

234
00:10:24,862 --> 00:10:26,320
You're not going
to write about it.

235
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:27,862
You're not going to
build a web page.

236
00:10:27,862 --> 00:10:30,490
You're not going to say
you're changing the world

237
00:10:30,490 --> 00:10:31,990
or helping to achieve something.

238
00:10:31,990 --> 00:10:33,365
You're not going
to set a record.

239
00:10:33,365 --> 00:10:35,530
You're just using your
airplane for transportation.

240
00:10:35,530 --> 00:10:36,970
You want to get to Oshkosh.

241
00:10:36,970 --> 00:10:38,410
You have an airplane.

242
00:10:38,410 --> 00:10:41,320
So why should the fact that
you're on a different continent

243
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:43,510
stop you from doing it?

244
00:10:43,510 --> 00:10:47,020
A lot of people have stopped
doing home projects, home

245
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:48,010
repairs.

246
00:10:48,010 --> 00:10:51,730
So Oshkosh, they actually
have people that teach kids

247
00:10:51,730 --> 00:10:54,850
how to hammer, how to drill,
how to cut sheet metal.

248
00:10:54,850 --> 00:10:58,460
There's a lot of great
workshops there for kids.

249
00:10:58,460 --> 00:11:02,670
We were over by the Pioneer
Airport and the Museum.

250
00:11:02,670 --> 00:11:05,600
Here is the museum.

251
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:08,140
They have a replica
Wright Flyer, a whole area

252
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:11,770
devoted to the Rutan designs.

253
00:11:11,770 --> 00:11:13,718
I don't know how many--

254
00:11:13,718 --> 00:11:15,760
I don't know how many of
you know the MIT spinoff

255
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:16,870
Terrafugia.

256
00:11:16,870 --> 00:11:20,170
I guess-- I think it was
12-- at least 12 years ago,

257
00:11:20,170 --> 00:11:22,420
they said they were going
to build a flying car.

258
00:11:22,420 --> 00:11:26,320
So I took a picture of this
1949 Taylor Aerocar, which

259
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:30,560
is actually a
certificated flying car

260
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:36,600
and unfairly associated
with Terrafugia.

261
00:11:36,600 --> 00:11:41,010
So this guy is kind of
an inspiration, I think--

262
00:11:41,010 --> 00:11:43,590
Paul Poberezny, the founder
of the Experimental Aircraft

263
00:11:43,590 --> 00:11:44,760
Association.

264
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:47,790
He flew the most expensive and
highest-performance aircraft

265
00:11:47,790 --> 00:11:48,930
of the day.

266
00:11:48,930 --> 00:11:53,520
He flew the P-51 Mustang,
the P-38 Lightning.

267
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:55,480
And then he came
home and said, well,

268
00:11:55,480 --> 00:12:02,340
what's cool to do after flying a
12,000-pound-plus P-51 Mustang?

269
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:05,790
Why not just go to the
basement, get some plywood,

270
00:12:05,790 --> 00:12:09,872
nail it together, and build a
really crummy plywood airplane?

271
00:12:09,872 --> 00:12:10,830
And that's what he did.

272
00:12:10,830 --> 00:12:12,775
So it just shows the joy.

273
00:12:12,775 --> 00:12:14,775
You don't have to have a
high-performance, state

274
00:12:14,775 --> 00:12:16,157
of the art airplane.

275
00:12:16,157 --> 00:12:18,240
If you build it yourself
or you're goofing around,

276
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:18,960
you can have fun.

277
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:19,500
And he did.

278
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:24,570
And he was a better
pilot than [INAUDIBLE],,

279
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:26,490
I'm sure, in his P-51.

280
00:12:26,490 --> 00:12:31,610
So that made me think maybe we
shouldn't be snobby about these

281
00:12:31,610 --> 00:12:34,370
home-built airplanes, because
if it was good enough--

282
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:35,770
Poberezny had fun with it.

283
00:12:35,770 --> 00:12:36,270
All right.

284
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:38,460
So here's some examples
of home-builts.

285
00:12:38,460 --> 00:12:42,490
Up on the left there, there's
some kind of canard velocity.

286
00:12:42,490 --> 00:12:47,180
These folks spent 15 years
building this helicopter.

287
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:49,500
And they had about 40
hours of flight time on it.

288
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:53,640
So to me, in a good
month at a flight school,

289
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:58,070
a Robinson helicopter might
fly 90 hours or 100 hours

290
00:12:58,070 --> 00:12:58,800
in just a month.

291
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:03,390
So it's taken them 15 years
plus to get to where a flight

292
00:13:03,390 --> 00:13:05,580
school would get in a month.

293
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,350
But that's kind
of not the point.

294
00:13:07,350 --> 00:13:09,060
It's not about
efficiency apparently.

295
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:10,525
That's Rotorway kit, I bet.

296
00:13:10,525 --> 00:13:12,900
And they modified it by sticking
in a little tiny turbine

297
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:13,640
engine.

298
00:13:13,640 --> 00:13:14,920
This is another modification.

299
00:13:14,920 --> 00:13:18,240
This is an old Rutan design
that became the Berkut

300
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:19,740
with a big piston engine.

301
00:13:19,740 --> 00:13:23,620
This guy stuck a turbo
jet engine in it.

302
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:25,470
Here's the company--
yeah, 42,000--

303
00:13:25,470 --> 00:13:27,990
I think that doesn't include
the engine, which is under 50.

304
00:13:27,990 --> 00:13:31,360
You can build your own
little single-seat jet.

305
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:32,310
Cirrus was there.

306
00:13:32,310 --> 00:13:37,510
Cirrus actually has been kind
of an industry-changing company.

307
00:13:37,510 --> 00:13:39,010
It didn't seem that
way at the time,

308
00:13:39,010 --> 00:13:40,990
because it's very
similar in performance

309
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:43,720
to Mooneys and
Pipers, and Cessnas.

310
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:46,360
But they really have
revolutionized the way

311
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:49,030
that families buy airplanes now.

312
00:13:49,030 --> 00:13:51,210
And they've got
over 7,000 of them.

313
00:13:51,210 --> 00:13:54,820
The parachute is, I
think, critical to getting

314
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:57,760
modern people to
accept this design.

315
00:13:57,760 --> 00:13:59,920
And also the glass cockpit
does make life easier.

316
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:01,930
Although, everybody else
has caught up there.

317
00:14:01,930 --> 00:14:03,250
Here's the Icon A5.

318
00:14:03,250 --> 00:14:04,750
They have the same
booth every year.

319
00:14:04,750 --> 00:14:05,955
A huge amount of excitement.

320
00:14:05,955 --> 00:14:06,580
People love it.

321
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:08,050
A lot of people have ordered it.

322
00:14:08,050 --> 00:14:10,480
They haven't figured
out how to build it.

323
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:12,730
The price has gone
up to the point where

324
00:14:12,730 --> 00:14:15,970
you can almost by a Cessna
Caravan on floats with a jet

325
00:14:15,970 --> 00:14:18,600
engine for the same price.

326
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:21,310
Here is a retired Air Force
and Southwest Airlines

327
00:14:21,310 --> 00:14:24,170
pilot with his electric
self-launching Pipistrel

328
00:14:24,170 --> 00:14:24,680
glider.

329
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:26,530
Pipistrel is the
company to watch.

330
00:14:26,530 --> 00:14:28,570
They do a lot of
interesting stuff

331
00:14:28,570 --> 00:14:32,998
in Slovenia, especially
with electrics.

332
00:14:32,998 --> 00:14:34,540
This is not the kind
of radar picture

333
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:39,040
you want to see when
you're in a $99 tent.

334
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:44,110
I will say to Costco that it
did hold up remarkably well.

335
00:14:44,110 --> 00:14:46,280
But it was scary.

336
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:48,940
The seaplane base a few
miles away from Oshkosh.

337
00:14:48,940 --> 00:14:51,670
They have the Oshkosh
seaplane base.

338
00:14:51,670 --> 00:14:54,428
I think it's on Lake Winnebago.

339
00:14:54,428 --> 00:14:56,470
So you take a shuttle bus
or you drive over there

340
00:14:56,470 --> 00:14:59,860
if you have a rental car.

341
00:14:59,860 --> 00:15:03,230
So you'll see-- they call it
the quiet part of Oshkosh.

342
00:15:03,230 --> 00:15:06,290
It's quiet because you only hear
about three or four aircraft

343
00:15:06,290 --> 00:15:08,810
engines running at any
one time and maybe two

344
00:15:08,810 --> 00:15:10,790
takeoffs or landings.

345
00:15:10,790 --> 00:15:13,130
They have four hangars, vendors.

346
00:15:13,130 --> 00:15:16,880
If you want to take your dog
in an unpressurized airplane up

347
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:20,090
to 18,000 feet, you
can buy this kit.

348
00:15:20,090 --> 00:15:24,220
I don't know what our golden
retriever would say about that.

349
00:15:24,220 --> 00:15:28,460
There's affinity groups
for different slices.

350
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:31,300
Here's a woman who just
like-- just for female pilots

351
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:33,260
to Mooneys.

352
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:35,510
Air shows every day.

353
00:15:35,510 --> 00:15:38,480
My favorite acts actually were--

354
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:39,620
I did like the GameBird.

355
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:40,380
That was cool.

356
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:42,350
But I also liked
these first two were

357
00:15:42,350 --> 00:15:44,480
kind of regular
family airplanes just

358
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:45,950
being pushed to their limits.

359
00:15:45,950 --> 00:15:48,727
It wouldn't be legal just to
go out and do that in a Baron,

360
00:15:48,727 --> 00:15:50,060
because you can't do acrobatics.

361
00:15:50,060 --> 00:15:53,045
But he'll put it into
experimental category,

362
00:15:53,045 --> 00:15:54,920
and then just fly it
right up to the G limits

363
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:56,600
for which it was designed.

364
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:59,630
And it's amazing to see
what these ordinary family

365
00:15:59,630 --> 00:16:03,020
or business airplanes
can do when pushed.

366
00:16:03,020 --> 00:16:05,750
There were-- one thing
that's interesting,

367
00:16:05,750 --> 00:16:08,942
about 150 Beechcraft
aerobatic Bonanzas were built.

368
00:16:08,942 --> 00:16:09,900
They were strengthened.

369
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:15,030
And it is legal to do aerobatics
in your four-seat Bonanza.

370
00:16:15,030 --> 00:16:16,920
Night airshow.

371
00:16:16,920 --> 00:16:18,660
I guess that's worth showing.

372
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:21,360
It is so unusual.

373
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:24,720
They had explosives and
fireworks mounted on aircraft.

374
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:28,370
They fly around at night,
shooting fireworks off.

375
00:16:28,370 --> 00:16:31,770
I guess you may have to
view that on your own,

376
00:16:31,770 --> 00:16:33,940
which isn't so bad.

377
00:16:33,940 --> 00:16:36,160
A lot of warbirds.

378
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:38,470
Everything from
World War II is still

379
00:16:38,470 --> 00:16:39,760
flying pretty much at Oshkosh.

380
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:43,988
That Grumman Tigercat
on the right,

381
00:16:43,988 --> 00:16:45,530
I'd never even heard
of that airplane

382
00:16:45,530 --> 00:16:46,613
until I saw it at Oshkosh.

383
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:51,490
That's-- I think this
is EAA's own B-17,

384
00:16:51,490 --> 00:16:54,890
which they fly around and
sell rides in and stuff.

385
00:16:54,890 --> 00:16:59,840
Oh, and up on the top right
is a flying 1/3 Scale B-17

386
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:06,520
that some crazy guy spent
15 years building or more.

387
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:07,244
That's EAA.

388
00:17:07,244 --> 00:17:09,619
Also, they have a B-29.

389
00:17:09,619 --> 00:17:12,547
Up there at the top right is my
friend Eric with his personal--

390
00:17:12,547 --> 00:17:14,839
I guess he's in the foundation
with a few other people,

391
00:17:14,839 --> 00:17:17,960
but essentially a personal DC-3.

392
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:19,550
Oh, and he's flying
that to Europe

393
00:17:19,550 --> 00:17:23,826
this year for the 50th
Anniversary of D-day.

394
00:17:23,826 --> 00:17:25,810
Is it the 50th?

395
00:17:25,810 --> 00:17:27,330
No, it can't be.

396
00:17:27,330 --> 00:17:28,190
AUDIENCE: 75th.

397
00:17:28,190 --> 00:17:28,569
PHILIP GREENSPUN: What?

398
00:17:28,569 --> 00:17:29,310
75th.

399
00:17:29,310 --> 00:17:30,130
Sorry.

400
00:17:30,130 --> 00:17:30,640
Thank you.

401
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:33,670
I was a math undergrad.

402
00:17:33,670 --> 00:17:36,587
Good illustration of the
value of a Course 18 degree.

403
00:17:36,587 --> 00:17:38,170
And actually, when
I was an undergrad,

404
00:17:38,170 --> 00:17:39,587
people said, how
will you ever get

405
00:17:39,587 --> 00:17:41,170
a job if you're a math major?

406
00:17:41,170 --> 00:17:42,540
The engineers all said that.

407
00:17:42,540 --> 00:17:44,810
Well, we said, well, we
can become actuaries.

408
00:17:44,810 --> 00:17:47,390
We can become actuaries.

409
00:17:47,390 --> 00:17:52,030
There was a group of folks who
were passionate about warbirds

410
00:17:52,030 --> 00:17:53,380
down in the south.

411
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:55,900
And they called
their organization

412
00:17:55,900 --> 00:17:58,098
the Confederate Air Force.

413
00:17:58,098 --> 00:17:59,640
Well, they've had
to change that now.

414
00:17:59,640 --> 00:18:01,980
Now they're the
Commemorative Air Force.

415
00:18:01,980 --> 00:18:03,550
And they're big into--

416
00:18:03,550 --> 00:18:05,320
they brought the
Tuskegee Airmen,

417
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:09,010
some of the remaining P-51
guys from World War II there.

418
00:18:09,010 --> 00:18:11,860
I gave a talk with NOAA.

419
00:18:11,860 --> 00:18:15,130
That's Kate from NOAA trying to
get volunteer pilots to fly sea

420
00:18:15,130 --> 00:18:18,250
turtles back from Cape Cod
and other northern places

421
00:18:18,250 --> 00:18:20,333
to Florida.

422
00:18:20,333 --> 00:18:21,875
Sunday morning, it
was time to leave.

423
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:28,820
For me, I saw 30% of
what we'd hoped to see

424
00:18:28,820 --> 00:18:31,190
and 1% of what there was to see.

425
00:18:31,190 --> 00:18:34,093
As you can see from the
right, that One Week Wonder's

426
00:18:34,093 --> 00:18:34,760
coming together.

427
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:36,800
It actually did fly,
I believe, on Sunday.

428
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:40,270
So they got it built
in seven or eight days.

429
00:18:40,270 --> 00:18:43,390
We went to Mackinac
Island, beautiful place up

430
00:18:43,390 --> 00:18:46,340
near the Canadian border.

431
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:48,050
Everything's horse-drawn
or a bicycle.

432
00:18:52,433 --> 00:18:53,850
A fun place to go
in the airplane.

433
00:18:53,850 --> 00:18:56,010
We went and landed in
the Toronto City Island

434
00:18:56,010 --> 00:18:58,890
Airport, which is right
in front of the downtown.

435
00:18:58,890 --> 00:19:02,910
We had Chinese food,
saw the aquarium,

436
00:19:02,910 --> 00:19:04,110
landed back in Bedford.

437
00:19:04,110 --> 00:19:07,800
So we burned off 15
hours of Hobbs time.

438
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:10,340
Split by three people, that
wouldn't be too horrible.

439
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:12,720
It was about $3,000 at retail.

440
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:16,710
Next year, I think it might
be time to go JetBlue.

441
00:19:16,710 --> 00:19:22,260
So the summary is that
even as the regulation

442
00:19:22,260 --> 00:19:26,850
has made light aircraft
almost as expensive to operate

443
00:19:26,850 --> 00:19:31,590
commercially at least or for
charter as the big aircraft,

444
00:19:31,590 --> 00:19:33,450
but Oshkosh keeps
growing every year.

445
00:19:33,450 --> 00:19:34,950
More people are
passionate about it.

446
00:19:34,950 --> 00:19:37,110
So what you're seeing
in the aircraft world

447
00:19:37,110 --> 00:19:39,070
is like the very high
end and the very low end

448
00:19:39,070 --> 00:19:42,050
are kind of prospering.

449
00:19:42,050 --> 00:19:43,482
This is Oxana's thing.

450
00:19:43,482 --> 00:19:45,690
She asked everybody like,
what brings you to Oshkosh?

451
00:19:45,690 --> 00:19:47,860
And they always
said, the people,

452
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:49,620
I love talking to
the people here.

453
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:51,690
And I feel that way, too.

454
00:19:51,690 --> 00:19:54,230
People go year after year.

455
00:19:54,230 --> 00:19:56,670
There's a whole bunch of links
here in the presentation.

456
00:19:56,670 --> 00:19:59,340
And finally, what I
like most about it is--

457
00:19:59,340 --> 00:20:03,210
and the reason I'm going back,
I think, is it's my safe space.

458
00:20:03,210 --> 00:20:05,050
Outside of this room
if I tell people

459
00:20:05,050 --> 00:20:07,260
I like to go over
to Hanscom Field

460
00:20:07,260 --> 00:20:10,950
and fly this little four-seat
airplane with a piston engine,

461
00:20:10,950 --> 00:20:11,730
they're horrified.

462
00:20:11,730 --> 00:20:13,660
They think that
that's just so dumb.

463
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:16,140
You could just drive in
New York in your Honda.

464
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:18,105
You could get a ticket
on JetBlue for no money.

465
00:20:18,105 --> 00:20:19,230
Like why would you do that?

466
00:20:19,230 --> 00:20:20,190
It's dumb.

467
00:20:20,190 --> 00:20:24,710
But Oshkosh is the one place
where nobody will say that.